    {'heading': 'Lesson II'},
    {'para': '**97. Verbs. Unaccented ⟨a⟩-class, cont’d.** Roots of this class which end in a vowel, and consonant-roots not forming heavy syllables (§ 53), gunate their vowels in forming their present-stems; e. g., ⟨ji⟩ and ⟨nī⟩ form ⟨je⟩ and ⟨ne⟩; ⟨dru⟩ and ⟨bhū⟩ form ⟨dro⟩ and ⟨bho⟩; ⟨smṛ⟩ forms ⟨smar⟩; ⟨cit⟩ and ⟨budh⟩ form ⟨cet⟩ and ⟨bodh⟩; ⟨vṛṣ⟩ forms ⟨varṣ⟩.'},
    {'para': '**98.** With the class-sign ⟨a⟩, a final ⟨e⟩ of the gunated root unites to form ⟨aya⟩ — see § 159; so ⟨o⟩ with ⟨a⟩ becomes ⟨ava⟩; ⟨ar⟩ with ⟨a⟩ yields ⟨ara⟩. Thus, ⟨ji⟩, 3rd sing. ⟨jayati⟩; ⟨bhū⟩ ⟨bhavati⟩; ⟨smṛ⟩ ⟨smarati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**99.** Roots in consonants: ⟨budh⟩, 3rd sing. ⟨bodhati⟩; ⟨cit⟩ ⟨cetati⟩; ⟨vṛṣ⟩ ⟨varṣati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**100.** The roots ⟨gam⟩ and ⟨yam⟩ make the present-stems ⟨gaccha⟩ and ⟨yaccha⟩∗.'},
    {'para': '**101.** The root ⟨sad⟩, ‘sit’, makes the present-stem ⟨sīda⟩. The root ⟨guh⟩, ‘hide’, makes ⟨gūhati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**102.** Several roots in final ⟨ā⟩ form their present-stem by a peculiar process of reduplication; thus, ⟨sthā⟩, 3rd sing. ⟨tiṣṭhati⟩∗∗; ⟨pā⟩ ⟨pibati⟩; ⟨ghrā⟩ ⟨jighrati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**103. Masculines and Neuters in ⟨a⟩.**'},
    {'para': 'a. Masculines: ⟨deva⟩, ‘god’.'},
