    {'para': '∗ As a rule, the grammarians do not allow ⟨ch⟩ to stand in that form after a vowel, but require it to be doubled, becoming ⟨cch⟩. An aspirate is doubled by prefixing the corresponding non-aspirate. Cf. § 165.'},
    {'para': '∗∗ The dental sibilant ⟨s⟩ is changed to the lingual ⟨ṣ⟩, if immediately preceded by any vowel save ⟨a⟩ and ⟨ā⟩, or by ⟨k⟩ or ⟨r⟩ — unless the ⟨s⟩ be final, or followed by ⟨r⟩. Thus, ⟨ti-stha-ti⟩ becomes ⟨tiṣṭhati⟩ (the change of ⟨th⟩ to ⟨ṭh⟩ — a process of assimilation — will be explained below). So ⟨agni-su⟩ becomes ⟨agniṣu⟩; and ⟨dhanus-ā⟩ becomes ⟨dhanuṣā⟩.'},
    {'para': 'The nasalization of the alterant vowel, or in other words, its being followed by *anusvāra*, does not prevent its altering effect upon the sibilant; thus, ⟨havīṃṣi⟩. And the alteration takes place in the initial of an ending after the final ⟨s⟩ of a stem, whether the latter be regarded as also changed to ⟨ṣ⟩ or as converted into *visarga*; thus, ⟨haviṣ-ṣu⟩ or ⟨haviḥ-ṣu⟩ instead of ⟨havis-su⟩.'},
    {'para': '**104. Force of cases.** 1. The nominative is *casus subjectivus*. 2. The accusative is *casus objectivus*, denoting chiefly the nearer or direct, sometimes however the more remote, object; sometimes also the *terminus ad quem*, and extent of time and space.'},
